meta { title = "Functions and closures", description = "Compose named functions, callbacks and captured environments.", order = 3, group = "Language reference" }

Use fn to declare a function, annotate its parameters and optionally write its result type after ->. Use return to produce a function result. Every completing path of a function with a non-unit result must return explicitly. Export a declaration when another module must import it.

h2 { "Return and completion" }

return exits the current function. It can return the result of an if, match or loop expression. The branches of those expressions retain their expression results.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/io" import println, IoError
from "std/fmt" import format

fn absolute(value: i64) -> i64:
  if value < 0:
    return -value
  return value

fn main() -> unit ! IoError:
  output: i64 = absolute(-42)

  try println(format("{}", output))
  return ()
""" }

This displays 42. Returning from an anonymous function exits that anonymous function. Enclosing task groups settle before the function finishes, and owned resources are released.

unit has one value, written (). A unit function can finish naturally or use return without an operand.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """fn doNothing() -> unit:
  return

fn main() -> unit:
  return doNothing()
""" }

This program completes successfully with no output.

h2 { "Functions as values" }

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/io" import println, IoError
from "std/fmt" import format

fn double(value: i64) -> i64:
  return value * 2

fn apply(value: i64, transform: fn(i64) -> i64) -> i64:
  return transform(value)

fn main() -> unit ! IoError:
  callback = double
  output: i64 = apply(21, callback)

  try println(format("{}", output))
  return ()
""" }

This displays 42. A function type records parameter types, result type and any expected-error channel. A function-valued binding can be passed, returned or invoked with ordinary call syntax.

h2 { "Capture a value" }

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/io" import println, IoError
from "std/fmt" import format

fn makeAdder(offset: i64) -> fn(i64) -> i64:
  return fn(value: i64): return value + offset

fn main() -> unit ! IoError:
  addTen = makeAdder(10)
  output: i64 = addTen(32)

  try println(format("{}", output))
  return ()
""" }

This displays 42. The returned closure retains its captured environment after makeAdder returns. Scalar captures are value snapshots. Anonymous bodies may also use an indented suite with local bindings. Rebinding a captured scalar changes only the current call's private copy. It does not update the captured environment, a later call, or the caller's binding. Array captures are read-only aliases to retained backing storage.

h2 { "Array access contracts" }

Ordinary array parameters and array captures provide read access. Declare a mut parameter when the helper needs to write its caller's numeric or bool array. Changing a parameter's local name is different from writing its array elements. A function type records the same access requirement, even if the function is stored in another binding or record field.

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/io" import println, IoError
from "std/fmt" import format

fn writableIdentity(mut values: [i64]) -> [i64]:
  return values

fn main() -> unit ! IoError:
  values = [1, 2, 3]
  forward: fn(mut [i64]) -> [i64] = writableIdentity
  output: i64 = length(forward(values))

  try println(format("{}", output))
  return ()
""" }

This example returns 3. Its function type preserves the mut requirement even though writableIdentity itself only returns the array. A fresh local array or an independent copy can satisfy a mut contract. An ordinary parameter or captured array cannot acquire write permission merely through a local alias, a function return or an indirect call. Use copy when a function needs independent writable storage.

Tasks receive parent-provided arrays read-only. A spawned call cannot request mut access to those arrays; it can create and edit its own private storage. While any unfinished task may read an array, mutating calls through every alias are rejected. Await every relevant reader before writing. Awaiting only one of several readers, awaiting in only one conditional branch, or awaiting inside a loop that may run zero times does not establish unconditional settlement. When two parameters might alias, the checker preserves the same protection.

h2 { "Callbacks and execution" }

map calls its callback sequentially. parallelMap schedules callbacks on CPU workers. Both accept checked function values and supported closures. A closure passed to a worker must capture values that can be shared with that worker. GPU tasks also accept CPU-created callbacks when the compiler can identify every possible target and all captured values are device-compatible. See the GPU numerical guide for capture types, ownership rules and examples.

h2 { "Recursive calls" }

codeblock { language = kalandra, """from "std/io" import println, IoError
from "std/fmt" import format

fn factorial(value: i64) -> i64:
  return if value <= 1:
    1
  else:
    value * factorial(value - 1)

fn main() -> unit ! IoError:
  output: i64 = factorial(5)

  try println(format("{}", output))
  return ()
""" }

This displays 120. Functions can call themselves and each other on the CPU, including in CPU tasks. Recursive calls consume execution resources; very deep recursion can exhaust them. Do not depend on tail-call elimination for correctness.

list {
  link { url = "/docs/arrays", "Map arrays sequentially" },
